Find out whether the Windows AutoHotkey v2 driver test suites can run on Linux under Wine, and if so, use that to validate Windows code from Linux. Context: the Windows driver is static/ergopti_plus/windows (AutoHotkey v2; find the pinned AHK version in the repo: grep for 2.0. in tools/ci, .github/workflows/ci-windows.yml, windows/README). Steps: install wine (wine64/wine-stable from apt, or WineHQ), download the pinned AutoHotkey v2 portable zip from the official GitHub release (github.com/AutoHotkey/AutoHotkey/releases) and check its SHA-256 against any pin in the repo; run the unit/meta suite the way ci-windows.yml does (windows/tests/run_all.ahk, with its --only filters and the per-category shards; read .agents/skills/ahk-driver/SKILL.md and docs/memory/windows-ahk.md first: run_all.ahk is #SingleInstance Force, never run two at once; never boot against a live config). Measure which test categories run correctly under Wine (pass/fail vs what CI reports for the same commit: you can read recent CI logs only if you have access; otherwise compare categories to their expected all-pass state). If a meaningful subset runs, write a small, documented runner `tools/test/run-ahk-under-wine.sh` (or .cjs, matching repo conventions, with a strict file header and the convention lint passing) that installs nothing by itself, takes the AHK exe path, runs a chosen category/--only filter and returns AHK's exit code, plus a docs/memory/windows-ahk.md entry stating exactly which families are reliable under Wine and which are not (hooks, SendInput, WebView2, COM are likely not). Then run the suite on the current dev and report every red test with your judgement: Wine artefact vs genuine Windows bug; fix only genuine bugs that you can prove (one commit each). Do not change Windows driver behaviour to suit Wine.
